{
  "id": "np-reshape-stack",
  "title": "Reshaping & Stacking",
  "icon": "🧩",
  "level": "beginner",
  "order": 20,
  "conceptRef": "numpy.html#s4",
  "blurb": "Same elements, new arrangement: reshape by reading order, then stack blocks along explicit axes.",
  "cells": [
    {
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "reshape re-labels, it never reorders",
      "body": "<p><code>reshape</code> never moves a byte. It keeps the elements in <b>reading order</b> (row-major, C order) and re-describes that same sequence with a new shape. That is why <code>a.reshape(3, 4)</code> and back <code>.reshape(12)</code> round-trips perfectly, and why reshapes are cheap: numpy builds a new header, not a copy — unless the elements cannot be viewed at the new strides, in which case numpy silently copies and you never notice a semantic difference.</p><p>The reasoning rule: after any reshape, element <code>k</code> of the flattened array is still the k-th element of the original. If you can argue from reading order, you can predict any chain of reshapes — including the image-to-patches trick at the end of this notebook. And the <code>-1</code> slot means 'numpy, you decide this one' while keeping the total count fixed.</p>",
      "demo": {
        "code": "import numpy as np\n\na = np.arange(12)\nprint(a.reshape(3, 4))\nprint(a.reshape(3, 4).ravel())          # same reading order as a\nprint(a.reshape(2, 6).reshape(3, 4))    # shape round-trip: identity\nprint(a.reshape(3, -1).shape)           # -1 = 'numpy decides'"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "Stacking joins along an existing axis",
      "body": "<p><code>vstack</code> stacks row blocks underneath each other (axis 0 grows); <code>hstack</code> places blocks side by side (axis 1 grows); <code>concatenate</code> is the general form with an explicit axis. All of them accept only blocks that <b>match on every other axis</b> — you cannot stack a 2×3 on a 2×4 vertically, because the column counts disagree.</p><p>So the planning move is to draw the target shape first, cut it into blocks, and note each block's required shape. Composition then falls out: an outer <code>vstack</code> of an inner <code>hstack</code> builds a panel with a footer strip in one expression. This is the array version of 'plan the layout before you glue' — and it works identically for 3-D batches, where axis 0 is the batch.</p>",
      "demo": {
        "code": "import numpy as np\n\ntop_l = np.arange(4).reshape(2, 2)\ntop_r = np.arange(10, 14).reshape(2, 2)\nfooter = np.full((1, 4), 99)\npanel = np.vstack([np.hstack([top_l, top_r]), footer])\nprint(panel.shape)\nprint(panel)"
      }
    },
