        "hints": [
          "What does the -1 in reshape(rows, -1) promise numpy? Given that the total element count is fixed, what is the only width numpy can choose?",
          "Do the reshape for real — b = np.asarray(a).reshape(rows, -1) — and then report b.shape. numpy has already applied the rule cols = a.size // rows for you.",
          "return [int(b.shape[0]), int(b.shape[1])] — a plain list of Python ints read off the reshaped result."
        ],
        "reasoning": "<p>The <code>-1</code> slot means 'whatever keeps the element count unchanged'. Since reshape preserves <code>size</code>, the only consistent width is <code>a.size // rows</code> — and numpy enforces it, raising when <code>rows</code> does not divide <code>a.size</code>. Reading the shape back from the reshape result, instead of doing the division yourself, keeps a <b>single source of truth</b>: if numpy's rule ever changed, your code could not silently drift from it. The same 'shape as data' habit applies anywhere an API needs an explicit layout: reshape with -1, then inspect <code>.shape</code> — never re-derive what the library already computed.</p>",
        "solution": "import numpy as np\n\ndef infer_shape(a, rows):\n    \"\"\"Return the shape of a.reshape(rows, -1) as [rows, cols].\"\"\"\n    b = np.asarray(a).reshape(rows, -1)\n    return [int(b.shape[0]), int(b.shape[1])]\n"
      }
