        "hints": [
          "Draw the target shape and mark what must agree before any glue: which dimension must match for a horizontal join, and which for the final vertical stack?",
          "First join left and right horizontally — np.hstack([left, right]) — which requires equal row counts. Then place that above bottom with np.vstack, which requires equal column counts.",
          "return np.vstack([np.hstack([left, right]), bottom]). The inner call makes (r, c1+c2); the outer appends bottom's k rows, giving (r+k, c1+c2)."
        ],
        "reasoning": "<p>Stacking calls are <b>shape contracts</b>: <code>hstack</code> demands equal rows (it grows axis 1), <code>vstack</code> demands equal columns (it grows axis 0). Order the joins so each contract is already satisfied: the inner <code>hstack</code> works because <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> share <code>r</code>; the outer <code>vstack</code> works because the joined top — now <code>(r, c1+c2)</code> — matches <code>bottom</code>'s width. Thinking 'which dimension does this join fold along, and which must it leave untouched' turns any block-layout problem — image panels, batch assembly, table composition — into a two-line composition. Because both calls copy, the result is a fresh array with a clean int64 dtype, never a view into the inputs.</p>",
        "solution": "import numpy as np\n\ndef assemble_panel(left, right, bottom):\n    \"\"\"Glue left|right horizontally, then stack the footer below.\"\"\"\n    return np.vstack([np.hstack([left, right]), bottom])\n"
