        "hints": [
          "A patch position is four sub-indices: (patch-row i, within-row a, patch-col j, within-col b). In what order do those four appear in the original (H, W) reading order, and in what order do you need them for (P, ph, pw)?",
          "First reshape (N, H, W) into (N, H//ph, ph, W//pw, pw): this names the sub-indices in the original order (i, a, j, b). Then transpose so patch positions (i, j) come before within-patch offsets (a, b).",
          "x = img.reshape(n, h//ph, ph, w//pw, pw); x = x.transpose(0, 1, 3, 2, 4); return x.reshape(n, (h//ph)*(w//pw), ph, pw). The final reshape copies once — that is fine."
        ],
        "reasoning": "<p>The trick rests on one fact: <b>reshape and transpose never move elements — they re-describe them</b>. Element (n, i*ph+a, j*pw+b) has a fixed position in reading order; the 5-D reshape just names that position with five coordinates (n, i, a, j, b). The transpose then permutes the <i>names</i> so patch coordinates (i, j) come before within-patch coordinates (a, b); the final reshape collapses (i, j) into one patch index <code>p = i*(W//pw) + j</code>, which is exactly the row-major patch order requested. Transposes are views, so the only copy happens in the last reshape — one copy for the whole batch, no Python-level iteration. This reshape-transpose-reshape pattern is the standard way to vectorize any tiling or windowing problem, from convolutions to attention blocks.</p>",
        "solution": "import numpy as np\n\ndef to_patches(img, ph, pw):\n    \"\"\"Cut (N, H, W) images into (N, P, ph, pw) patches.\"\"\"\n    n, h, w = img.shape\n    hp, wp = h // ph, w // pw\n    x = img.reshape(n, hp, ph, wp, pw)     # (n, i, a, j, b)\n    x = x.transpose(0, 1, 3, 2, 4)         # (n, i, j, a, b)\n    return x.reshape(n, hp * wp, ph, pw)   # (n, p, a, b)\n"
      }
